# ============================================================================
# zsh function library - v2.17
#
# v2.17:
#   - sshmount(): verifies the mount actually established after sshfs
#     returns. sshfs can exit 0 even when the backgrounded mount fails to
#     come up, leaving a stale empty mountpoint and a misleading success
#     message. macOS ships no `mountpoint` utility, so the check greps the
#     mount table (`mount | grep -qF " on <path> "`). On verification
#     failure the created mountpoint directory is removed as before.
#
# v2.16:
#   - encrypt(): the plaintext zip archive is now staged in ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}
#     rather than in a temporary directory beside the output. On SMB mounts
#     'rm -rf' can return success without unlinking, so an archive staged on
#     the share was left behind on every invocation - an unencrypted copy of
#     the entire source tree, accumulating one directory per run. TMPDIR is
#     local and honours removal. _gpg_publish()'s temporary directory still
#     lives beside the output, because the hard-link publish requires the
#     same filesystem, and that one is removed successfully.
#
# v2.15:
#   - encrypt() / _gpg_publish(): EXIT-trap cleanup now expands and quotes
#     the temporary path at trap-set time. zsh pops a function's local scope
#     before running its EXIT trap, so the previous single-quoted '$var'
#     expanded to empty and silently cleaned up nothing, leaving temporary
#     directories behind with no warning
#   - encrypt(): removes the plaintext archive directory explicitly on the
#     success path and disarms the EXIT trap once it is gone
#   - _gpg_publish(): success path now disarms the EXIT trap as well; the
#     previous temp_dir="" no longer suppresses it
#   - _archive_dir(): Info-ZIP rejects '--' ("can't use -- before archive
#     name"), so it is omitted. Option/operand ambiguity is handled by
#     construction instead: the archive path is always absolute (mktemp -d)
#     and the source operand is ./-prefixed so a directory name beginning
#     with '-' cannot be parsed as a flag
#
# v2.14:
#   - encrypt(): now accepts a directory as well as a regular file. A
#     directory is archived with zip into a private mode-0700 temporary
#     directory and encrypted from there, so no intermediate .zip is ever
#     written next to the original and no namespace collision is possible.
#     Output is <dir>.zip.<timestamp>.asc, so decrypt() restores <dir>.zip.
#     Requires zip only when a directory is passed.
#   - _archive_dir(): new internal helper wrapping the zip invocation
#     (stored paths are relative to the parent directory, so extraction
#     recreates the directory itself rather than an absolute path chain)
#
# v2.13:
#   - decrypt(): unrecognised file extensions now fail immediately with a
#     descriptive message listing accepted extensions, instead of falling
#     through to a misleading "won't overwrite input" error
#   - entropy(): output now includes a " bits" label and is formatted to
#     two decimal places (e.g. "131.09 bits")
#   - blastem: added APFS copy-on-write limitation note (rm/shred do not
#     guarantee physical erasure; FileVault is the real control)
#
# v2.12:
#   - openpgpkey_lookup_validate: detect GNU vs BSD base64 automatically
#     (GNU uses -d, BSD uses -D) so the function works regardless of whether
#     coreutils gnubin is on PATH
#   - e() renamed to entropy() to avoid PATH/alias collisions
#
# v2.11:
#   - openpgpkey_lookup: normalise OPENPGPKEY RDATA to a single Base64 line
#     and enable pipefail so dig failures are still propagated
#   - openpgpkey_lookup_validate: consume the normalised lookup output directly
#
# v2.10:
#   - openpgpkey_lookup_validate: decode and validate OPENPGPKEY DNS key
#     material, then confirm each primary key is present in the local GPG
#     keyring; requires base64, gpg, mktemp, and awk in addition to the
#     openpgpkey_lookup dependencies
#
# v2.9:
#   - openpgpkey_lookup: look up an OPENPGPKEY DNS record for an email
#     address; local-part is lowercased and hashed with SHA-256 (first 56
#     hex characters) per RFC 7929; requires dig and sha256sum
#
# v2.8:
#   - _gpg_publish: falls back to an O_EXCL noclobber copy when hard links
#     are unsupported (SMB, exFAT); no-overwrite guarantee preserved
#
# v2.7:
#   - generate_modhex / generate_modhex_bulk: numeric arguments are converted
#     with an explicit 10# base so leading zeros can never be re-interpreted
#   - _gpg_publish: requires at least one GPG option after the fixed
#     arguments; chmod/ln deliberately omit -- (unsupported by BSD versions;
#     operands are always absolute paths)
#   - verify-sig: rejects more than two arguments with a usage error
#   - release_card: comment clarifies that component termination failures are
#     not propagated but missing dependencies still fail
#   - myip: rejects carriage returns instead of silently removing them
# ============================================================================

